PowerShell IP Ping Checker

A simple PowerShell script that reads a list of IP addresses from a text file, tests their connectivity using ICMP Ping, and saves the results to an output text file.
Features

    Reads IP addresses from ips.txt
    Pings each IP address once
    Displays whether each IP is reachable or unreachable
    Saves the results to ping-results.txt
    Ignores empty lines
    Uses a simple IP | Status format

Requirements

    Windows PowerShell 5.1 or PowerShell 7+
    Network access to the target IP addresses
    ICMP traffic must be allowed by the target device or firewall

File Structure

Place the following files in the same directory:

                                                                    text
.
├── Ping-IPs.ps1
└── ips.txt

Input File

Create a file named ips.txt and add one IP address per line:

                                                                    text
8.8.8.8
192.168.1.1
192.168.1.10

Empty lines are ignored.
Usage

    Download or clone this repository.
    Open the ips.txt file.
    Add the IP addresses you want to test.
    Right-click Ping-IPs.ps1.
    Select Run with PowerShell.

The script creates a file named ping-results.txt in the same directory.
Example Output

                                                                    text
IP | Status
8.8.8.8 | Reachable
192.168.1.1 | Reachable
192.168.1.10 | Unreachable

Execution Policy

If PowerShell prevents the script from running, open PowerShell as a user and run:

                                                                    powershell
Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser RemoteSigned

Confirm the change if prompted, then run the script again.

Alternatively, you can run the script without changing the permanent execution policy:

                                                                    powershell
powershell.exe -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\Ping-IPs.ps1

Notes

    Reachable means that a Ping response was received.
    Unreachable means that no Ping response was received.
    An unreachable result does not necessarily mean that the device is offline. The device or firewall may be blocking ICMP traffic.
    The output file is overwritten each time the script runs.

